= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a differentiable <vector field> in <Cartesian coordinates>, the <divergence> and <curl> are
$$
\nabla\cdot F=\partial_iF_i,\qquad(\nabla\times F)_i=\epsilon_{ijk}\partial_jF_k,
$$
with summation over repeated indices. In components the curl is $(\partial_yF_z-\partial_zF_y,\partial_zF_x-\partial_xF_z,\partial_xF_y-\partial_yF_x)$. The <contraction of two Levi-Civita symbols> and the <product rule> give
$$
\begin{aligned}
[\nabla\times(F\times G)]_i
&=\epsilon_{ijk}\epsilon_{k\ell m}\partial_j(F_\ell G_m)\\
&=(\delta_{i\ell}\delta_{jm}-\delta_{im}\delta_{j\ell})\partial_j(F_\ell G_m)\\
&=G_j\partial_jF_i+F_i\partial_jG_j-G_i\partial_jF_j-F_j\partial_jG_i.
\end{aligned}
$$
Recognizing the <directional derivatives> gives the <curl of a cross product> identity
$$
\nabla\times(F\times G)=(G\cdot\nabla)F-G(\nabla\cdot F)-(F\cdot\nabla)G+F(\nabla\cdot G).
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <divergence> is $y^2+z^2+x^2$. Over the cube $[-1/2,1/2]^3$, each squared-coordinate term has integral $\int_{-1/2}^{1/2}s^2ds=1/12$, while the other two coordinate integrals have length one. Thus
$$
\boxed{\int_V\nabla\cdot F\,dV=3\cdot\frac1{12}=\frac14.}
$$
Compute the surface <flux> separately. On $x=1/2$, the outward normal is $e_x$ and $F\cdot n=y^2/2$, giving flux $1/24$. On $x=-1/2$, the normal is $-e_x$, so $F\cdot n=-xy^2=y^2/2$, again giving $1/24$. The two $y$ faces similarly give $z^2/2$ each, and the two $z$ faces give $x^2/2$ each. All six face integrals therefore give
$$
\boxed{\int_{\partial V}F\cdot dS=6\cdot\frac1{24}=\frac14.}
$$
The independently evaluated values agree, verifying the <divergence theorem> for this field and cube.

= 2
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Use <separation of variables> $u=X(x)T(t)$. The fixed-end <Dirichlet boundary conditions> select $X_n=\sin(nx)$, $n\geq1$, and each mode satisfies $T_n''+2hT_n'+n^2T_n=0$. In the physical resistance range $0\leq h<1$, all <normal modes> are underdamped, with $\omega_n=\sqrt{n^2-h^2}>0$. The modal factor normalized by $T_n(0)=1$, $T_n'(0)=0$ is
$$
T_n(t)=e^{-ht}\left[\cos(\omega_nt)+\frac h{\omega_n}\sin(\omega_nt)\right].
$$
Consequently the <linearly damped string> has solution
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty b_n\sin(nx)e^{-ht}\left[\cos(\omega_nt)+\frac h{\omega_n}\sin(\omega_nt)\right],\quad b_n=\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi f(x)\sin(nx)dx.}
$$
The coefficients are those of the initial <Fourier sine series>. Differentiating each normalized modal factor at zero confirms that the string starts from rest. Suitable initial data give convergence in the string's energy space; the piecewise linear profile here need not give a twice differentiable solution along every characteristic from its corner.

For the specified tent profile, integrating its two linear pieces by parts gives
$$
\int_0^\pi f(x)\sin(nx)dx=\frac{2}{n^2}\sin\frac{n\pi}{2}.
$$
For example the first piece gives $-\pi\cos(n\pi/2)/(2n)+\sin(n\pi/2)/n^2$, and the second gives the opposite cosine term and the same sine term. Hence
$$
\boxed{b_n=\frac4{\pi n^2}\sin\frac{n\pi}{2},\qquad b_{2m}=0,\quad b_{2m+1}=\frac{4(-1)^m}{\pi(2m+1)^2}.}
$$
The zero even coefficients also follow from reflection symmetry about the midpoint. This is the <Fourier sine series of a symmetric tent>. If $h<1$ were read without the physical assumption $h\geq0$, the same modal solution extends by replacing imaginary-frequency sine and cosine by their hyperbolic counterparts, and taking $\sin(\omega t)/\omega=t$ when $\omega=0$.

\Image[paper-1-damped-tent-string.png]
{title=Evolution of a tent-shaped string released from rest with linear damping}

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The homogeneous <Cauchy-Euler equation> has solutions $x$ and $x^{-1}$. Choose $y_L=x$, which vanishes at zero, and $y_R=x-x^{-1}$, which vanishes at one. Their <Wronskian> is $W(s)=y_L(s)y_R'(s)-y_L'(s)y_R(s)=2/s$. For a <Green function> integrated against the unweighted measure $ds$, continuity at $x=s$ and the jump condition $s^2[G_x]_{s^-}^{s^+}=1$ give
$$
\boxed{G(x,s)=\begin{cases}
\dfrac{x}{2}(1-s^{-2}),&0<x\leq s<1,\\[4pt]
\dfrac12(x-x^{-1}),&0<s\leq x<1.
\end{cases}}
$$
It satisfies $L_xG=\delta(x-s)$ for $L=x^2\partial_x^2+x\partial_x-1$. Therefore the candidate solution is
$$
\boxed{u(x)=\frac{x-x^{-1}}2\int_0^xf(s)ds+\frac x2\int_x^1(1-s^{-2})f(s)ds.}
$$
The leading coefficient vanishes at zero, so the endpoint condition needs care. If $f$ extends continuously to $[0,1]$, this expression has limit $-f(0)$ as $x\downarrow0$. Indeed $x^{-1}\int_0^xf(s)ds\to f(0)$ and $x\int_x^1f(s)s^{-2}ds\to f(0)$, while the remaining terms vanish. Thus the continuous zero-endpoint problem in this class requires **$f(0)=0$**. With that compatibility condition the formula gives both boundary values and is unique: a difference of two bounded solutions is $Ax+B/x$, whose two endpoint conditions force both constants to vanish. For incompatible forcing such as $f\equiv1$, the bounded solution with $u(1)=0$ is $x-1$, which cannot also vanish at zero. Pointwise vanishing of $G(x,s)$ for fixed $s$ does not justify interchanging the singular endpoint limit with its integral. This issue is treated in <Green kernel and endpoint compatibility for a singular Euler equation>.

For the supplied compatible forcing $f(s)=s$, the integrals are $x^2/2$ and $(1-x^2)/2+\log x$. Their polynomial pieces cancel, giving
$$
\boxed{u(x)=\frac{x\log x}{2}.}
$$
Direct differentiation gives $Lu=x$, and $x\log x\to0$ at zero. Its derivative diverges there, which is allowed because the boundary condition requires the value, not a finite derivative.

= 4
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Choose the <Fourier transform> convention $\widehat f(k)=\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-ikx}f(x)dx$, with inverse $(2\pi)^{-1}\int e^{ikx}\widehat f(k)dk$. For absolutely integrable $f_1,f_2$, <Fubini's theorem> permits the substitution $s=x-y$ in the convolution transform:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\widehat{f_1*f_2}(k)
&=\int_{\mathbb R}\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-ikx}f_1(y)f_2(x-y)dy\,dx\\
&=\left[\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-iky}f_1(y)dy\right]\left[\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-iks}f_2(s)ds\right].
\end{aligned}
$$
This proves the <convolution theorem> in the chosen convention.

Take $q(x)=\tfrac12e^{-|x|}$. Splitting its transform at zero gives $\widehat q(k)=\tfrac12[(1+ik)^{-1}+(1-ik)^{-1}]=(1+k^2)^{-1}$. Thus the desired inverse is $q*q$. For $x\geq0$, split the convolution integral at $y=0$ and $y=x$:
$$
(q*q)(x)=\frac14\left[\frac{e^{-x}}2+xe^{-x}+\frac{e^{-x}}2\right]=\frac14(1+x)e^{-x}.
$$
The result is even, so
$$
\boxed{\mathcal F^{-1}\!\left[\frac1{(1+k^2)^2}\right](x)=\frac14(1+|x|)e^{-|x|}.}
$$
Different normalization conventions alter the transform prefactors, which is why the convention was stated explicitly.

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

The <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices> supplies an <orthonormal basis> of real <eigenvectors>. Put these in the columns of $Q$, so $Q^TQ=I$ and $Q^TAQ=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n)$. In the question's coordinate convention choose $O=Q^T$ and $y=Ox$. Since $x=O^Ty$,
$$
x^TAx=y^TOAO^Ty=\sum_{i=1}^n\lambda_i y_i^2.
$$
Reorder the <eigenvalues> with $m$ positive, $\ell-m$ negative and $n-\ell$ zero. For nonzero eigenvalues set $z_i=\sqrt{|\lambda_i|}y_i$, and retain the remaining coordinates as $z_i=y_i$. This is an invertible real transformation $z=DOx$, where $D$ is the diagonal rescaling; the combined matrix $L=DO$ gives $z=Lx$. It gives
$$
x^TAx=\sum_{i=1}^mz_i^2-\sum_{i=m+1}^{\ell}z_i^2,
$$
with the zero coordinates absent. The general allowed range is **$0\leq m\leq\ell\leq n$**. The printed restriction $m\geq1$ is not valid for every real symmetric matrix: a negative-definite or zero form has no positive square. The invariance of these counts is <Sylvester's law of inertia>.

For the specified form, a particularly simple change is
$$
\boxed{z=Lx,\quad L=\begin{pmatrix}1/2&1/2&1/2\\1/2&-1/2&-1/2\\0&1&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad x_1x_2+x_1x_3=z_1^2-z_2^2.}
$$
Subtracting the first two squared coordinates verifies the identity directly, and $\det L=1$ proves invertibility. Thus $m=1$, $\ell=2$ and there is one zero direction. The final rescaling need not remain orthogonal.

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

A square <complex matrix> $U$ is a <unitary matrix> when $U^\dagger U=I$, so it preserves the <Euclidean norm>. If $Uv=\mu v$ with $v\ne0$, then $\|v\|=\|Uv\|=|\mu|\|v\|$, giving **$|\mu|=1$**. For a <Hermitian matrix> $A=A^\dagger$ and $Ae_j=\lambda_je_j$,
$$
\lambda_j=\frac{e_j^\dagger Ae_j}{e_j^\dagger e_j}\in\mathbb R,
$$
because the numerator equals its complex conjugate. Hence no eigenvalue of $A+iI$ is zero, proving it is invertible.

For $V=(A-iI)(A+iI)^{-1}$, the <Hermitian conjugate> is $V^\dagger=(A-iI)^{-1}(A+iI)$. The factors and their inverses commute, since they are rational functions of $A$. Thus $V^\dagger V=I$. Its action on the same <eigenvectors> is
$$
\boxed{Ve_j=\frac{\lambda_j-i}{\lambda_j+i}e_j.}
$$
These eigenvalues have modulus one. This is the <Cayley transform of a Hermitian matrix> in the convention that avoids the unitary eigenvalue one.

For the inverse direction, $U^\dagger=U^{-1}$ gives
$$
(I-U^{-1})^{-1}=-(I-U)^{-1}U,\qquad I+U^{-1}=U^{-1}(I+U).
$$
Taking the conjugate transpose of $B=i(I+U)(I-U)^{-1}$ therefore yields
$$
B^\dagger=-i(I-U^{-1})^{-1}(I+U^{-1})=i(I-U)^{-1}(I+U)=B.
$$
The last equality uses commutativity of the two factors. This proves that the <inverse Cayley transform of a unitary matrix without eigenvalue one> is Hermitian. Solving for $U$ gives $U=(B-iI)(B+iI)^{-1}$. An orthonormal <eigenbasis> of $B$ therefore diagonalizes $U$, with diagonal entries $(b_j-i)/(b_j+i)$.

The result extends even when a unitary matrix has eigenvalue one: choose $|\alpha|=1$ so that $\alpha U$ does not. There are only finitely many forbidden phases, since the matrix has finitely many <eigenvalues>. Diagonalizing $\alpha U$ also diagonalizes $U$ in the same basis. Thus **every unitary matrix is unitarily diagonalizable**.

