= Solution
{parent=a}

Use $\pi/12=\pi/3-\pi/4$ to multiply the elementary complex exponentials:
$$
2\sqrt2e^{i\pi/12}=2\sqrt2\left(\frac12+\frac{i\sqrt3}{2}\right)\left(\frac1{\sqrt2}-\frac i{\sqrt2}\right).
$$
Collecting real and imaginary parts gives
$$
\boxed{2\sqrt2e^{i\pi/12}=(1+\sqrt3)+i(\sqrt3-1).}
$$

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Factor $1-z^3=(1-z)(1+z+z^2)$. The <geometric series> gives, for $|z|<1$,
$$
\frac1{1+z+z^2}=\frac{1-z}{1-z^3}=1-z+z^3-z^4+z^6-z^7+\cdots.
$$
Thus the first three nonzero terms are **$1-z+z^3$**. If “first three terms” means the coefficients through degree two, that Taylor polynomial is $1-z+0z^2$. The distinction matters because the quadratic coefficient vanishes. The nearest singularities are the two nontrivial cube <roots of unity>, so the <radius of convergence> is exactly one.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $w=z-\pi$. The identity $\cosh^2(1/w)=[1+\cosh(2/w)]/2$ gives a <Laurent series> containing a constant and only even negative powers $w^{-2},w^{-4},\ldots$. There is no $w^{-1}$ coefficient, so
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Res}_{z=\pi}\cosh^2\!\left(\frac1{z-\pi}\right)=0.}
$$
The singularity is an <essential singularity>, not a pole: infinitely many negative Laurent coefficients are nonzero. The residue is still defined as the coefficient of $w^{-1}$.

= d
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $z=re^{i\theta}\ne0$, fix one argument $\theta$. Every value of the <complex logarithm> is $\log r+i(\theta+2\pi k)$, $k\in\mathbb Z$. By <complex exponentiation>, $z^z=\exp[z(\log r+i(\theta+2\pi k))]$. Taking the real part of its exponent gives all possible <moduli>:
$$
\boxed{|z^z|=\exp\!\left[r\cos\theta\log r-r\sin\theta(\theta+2\pi k)\right],\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
Equivalently these are $r^{\operatorname{Re}z}e^{-\operatorname{Im}z(\theta+2\pi k)}$. If $z$ is real and nonzero the modulus is the single value $r^{\operatorname{Re}z}$; otherwise the values form a geometric progression with ratio $e^{-2\pi\operatorname{Im}z}$. Changing the initially chosen argument just reindexes the same set. The logarithmic definition excludes $z=0$. This is the <modulus of a complex self-power>.

