= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a nonzero solution on a branch where the real power is differentiable, set $v=u^{1-\alpha}$. Multiplying the <Bernoulli differential equation> by $(1-\alpha)u^{-\alpha}$ gives the linear equation
$$
v'+(1-\alpha)f(x)v=(1-\alpha)g(x).
$$
It can be solved by an <integrating factor>. For the specified cubic equation take $\alpha=3$, $f=g=x$, so $v=u^{-2}$ satisfies $v'-2xv=-2x$. Its integrating factor is $e^{-x^2}$, giving $(ve^{-x^2})'=-2xe^{-x^2}$ and hence $v=1+Ce^{x^2}$. Thus all nonzero real solutions are
$$
\boxed{u(x)=\pm\frac1{\sqrt{1+Ce^{x^2}}},}
$$
on connected intervals where the denominator is positive, with a fixed sign on each interval. Also **$u\equiv0$** solves the original equation and must be restored because the substitution excluded it. The choices $C=0$ give the constant solutions $u=\pm1$. Smoothness of the original right-hand side gives uniqueness, so a nonzero solution cannot cross through the zero solution at a finite regular point.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write the analytic solution as $w(z)=\sum_{n\geq0}a_nz^n$. The constant term in the differential equation gives $a_1=0$, and matching the coefficient of $z^{n+1}$ gives
$$
(n+2)^2a_{n+2}+4a_n=0,\qquad n\geq0.
$$
All odd coefficients therefore vanish; $a_2=-a_0$ and $a_4=a_0/4$. The first three nonzero terms are
$$
\boxed{w(z)=a_0\left(1-z^2+\frac{z^4}{4}+O(z^6)\right).}
$$
In full, $a_{2j}=(-1)^ja_0/(j!)^2$, so $w=a_0J_0(2z)$, a <Bessel function of the first kind>. The <ratio test> proves convergence for every complex $z$. The other independent solution has a logarithmic singularity at zero and does not satisfy the analyticity requirement.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

Introduce a <Lagrange multiplier> for the constraint $G=1$ and vary $F-\lambda G$ with $y\mapsto y+\epsilon\eta$, where $\eta(a)=\eta(b)=0$. The first variation is
$$
\delta(F-\lambda G)=2\int_a^b[py'\eta'+(q-\lambda w)y\eta]dx
=2\int_a^b[-(py')'+qy-\lambda wy]\eta\,dx.
$$
The <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations> gives the <Sturm-Liouville equation> $-(py')'+qy=\lambda wy$. Multiplying by $y$, integrating by parts and using the endpoint conditions gives $F=\lambda G$. Therefore the normalization implies **$\lambda=F$**. Assume the coefficient and function regularity needed for these variations and integrations; the condition $w>0$ makes the constraint derivative nonzero at a normalized function.

For the radial problem, set $\sigma=r\psi$. A direct differentiation gives $\sigma''=r\psi''+2\psi'$, so
$$
-\sigma''=E\sigma,\qquad\sigma(0)=\sigma(1)=0.
$$
Finite $\psi(0)$ supplies the first boundary value. The <Rayleigh quotient> is $R[\sigma]=\int_0^1\sigma'^2dr/\int_0^1\sigma^2dr$. For the two trials, $\sigma_1=r(1-r)$ and $\sigma_2=r(1-r^2)$, the numerator-denominator pairs are $(1/3,1/30)$ and $(4/5,8/105)$, respectively. Hence
$$
\boxed{E_1=10,\qquad E_2=\frac{21}{2}.}
$$
The exact eigenvalues are $n^2\pi^2$, so $E_{\min}=\pi^2\simeq9.86960$. Both trials give upper bounds by the <Rayleigh-Ritz variational principle>, and **the first estimate is more accurate**, since it is smaller. Trial admissibility uses the Dirichlet energy form for $\sigma$; the first trial's nonzero radial derivative at the origin does not invalidate this variational calculation.

To improve, add independent trial functions and minimize the quotient over their span. Here merely combining the two supplied trials gives no improvement: their two-dimensional stiffness and mass matrices are
$$
K=\begin{pmatrix}1/3&1/2\\1/2&4/5\end{pmatrix},\quad
M=\begin{pmatrix}1/30&1/20\\1/20&8/105\end{pmatrix},\quad
\det(K-EM)=\frac{(E-10)(E-42)}{25200}.
$$
The lowest Ritz value is still ten. Instead add the symmetric quartic $\tau=r^2(1-r)^2$. On the span of $\sigma_1,\tau$, the <generalized eigenvalue problem> has
$$
K=\begin{pmatrix}1/3&1/15\\1/15&2/105\end{pmatrix},\qquad
M=\begin{pmatrix}1/30&1/140\\1/140&1/630\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Its determinant is $(E^2-112E+1008)/529200$, so the least root gives
$$
\boxed{E_{\rm improved}=56-4\sqrt{133}\simeq9.86974962.}
$$
This is strictly better than both original estimates. The <symmetry obstruction in a polynomial Rayleigh-Ritz trial space> explains why the cubic addition failed while the quartic helps. It is also the shifted-interval version of the <polynomial Rayleigh-Ritz trial space for a one-dimensional box>.

\Image[paper-1-rayleigh-ritz-trials.png]
{title=Original and improved polynomial trial functions compared with the lowest sine eigenfunction}

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

At a regular constrained extremum, the method of <Lagrange multipliers> makes $I+\lambda J$ stationary for a constant $\lambda$. For an endpoint-vanishing variation $\eta$, integration by parts gives
$$
\delta(I+\lambda J)=\int_{x_1}^{x_2}\left[f_y+\lambda g_y-\frac d{dx}(f_{y'}+\lambda g_{y'})\right]\eta\,dx.
$$
The <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations> therefore yields
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dx}(f_{y'}+\lambda g_{y'})-(f_y+\lambda g_y)=0.}
$$
This <Euler-Lagrange equation> must be supplemented by the two endpoint conditions and the prescribed integral constraint, which determine the constants and multiplier. Existence of the normal multiplier assumes that the first variation of $J$ is not identically zero. If that regularity fails, the general multiplier equation uses $\alpha I+\lambda J$, with $(\alpha,\lambda)\ne(0,0)$; one cannot automatically set $\alpha=1$.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write the speed as $v=\sqrt{\dot x^2+\dot y^2}>0$ and take
$$
\mathcal L=\frac12(x\dot y-y\dot x)+\lambda v.
$$
The two <Euler-Lagrange equations> are
$$
\boxed{\dot y-\lambda\frac d{dt}\left(\frac{\dot x}{v}\right)=0,\qquad
-\dot x-\lambda\frac d{dt}\left(\frac{\dot y}{v}\right)=0.}
$$
For example $\mathcal L_x=\dot y/2$ and $\mathcal L_{\dot x}=-y/2+\lambda\dot x/v$, yielding the first equation. Integrating once gives constants $c_1,c_2$ with $\lambda\dot x/v=y+c_1$ and $\lambda\dot y/v=-x+c_2$. The case $\lambda=0$ would force both velocities to vanish, contrary to a regular curve. Squaring and adding therefore gives
$$
\boxed{(x-c_2)^2+(y+c_1)^2=\lambda^2.}
$$
Thus every regular stationary curve is a circle, whose center is set by the integration constants. A simple closed traversal of length $L$ has radius $L/(2\pi)$ and positive enclosed area $L^2/(4\pi)$. The <planar isoperimetric inequality> proves that this stationary candidate is the global maximum; the necessary variational equations alone would not prove that inequality. The <stationary curves of the planar isoperimetric problem> give this argument independently of the chosen parametrization.
